const TEMPLES = [
  {"name":"Asuka-dera","kind":"temple","sect":"Sanron (historically); Shingon-shū Buzan-ha today","founded":"588","lat":34.4787,"lon":135.8202,"note":"Built as the family temple of the Soga clan, Asuka-dera (also Hōkō-ji) was the first Buddhist foundation in Japan to have a fully-fledged temple complex; the Nihon Shoki has Soga no Umako vow to raise it after defeating the anti-Buddhist Mononobe, records craftsmen and monks despatched from Baekje in 588, and notes its completion in 596. Its principal image, a bronze Shaka triad attributed to Kuratsukuri no Tori and finished between about 606 and 609, survives as the Asuka Daibutsu and is an Important Cultural Property. Primarily a Sanron centre, the temple drew immigrant clergy from Baekje, Goguryeo and China and functioned in effect as a research institute for Buddhist doctrine, which earned it imperial protection and let it outlive the destruction of the Soga in the Isshi Incident of 645. When the capital shifted to Heijō-kyō the institution moved with it and became Gangō-ji, while the Asuka site continued as Hon-Gangō-ji; it lost its pagoda and main hall to lightning in 1196 and was practically abandoned after the Muromachi period, its main image left standing in the open air by 1447. Excavation since 1956 has recovered the original one-pagoda, three-golden-hall plan, the present main hall of 1826 stands exactly on the site of the Central Golden Hall, and the precincts have been a National Historic Site since 1966.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Asuka-dera%2C_sanmon.jpg/960px-Asuka-dera%2C_sanmon.jpg","caption":"The sanmon gate of Asuka-dera in Asuka, Nara Prefecture. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Asuka-dera","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asuka-dera"}]},
  {"name":"Tōdai-ji","kind":"temple","sect":"Kegon","founded":"738 (Great Buddha dedicated 752)","lat":34.6954,"lon":135.845,"note":"Tōdai-ji was founded in 738 and opened in 752 as head of the provincial temple (kokubunji) network decreed by Emperor Shōmu in 741, after smallpox, failed harvests and the Fujiwara no Hirotsugu rebellion convinced him that state Buddhism might shield the realm. The colossal bronze Vairocana (Birushana) was commissioned in 743, built in eight castings over three years, completed in 751 and consecrated the following year at an eye-opening ceremony attended by 10,000 monks and 4,000 dancers, the rite performed by the Indian priest Bodhisena; temple records claim more than 2,600,000 people contributed rice, timber, metal, cloth or labour. Under the Ritsuryō state the temple housed offices of all six Nara schools and every officially licensed monk took ordination here, Kūkai and Saichō among them, until the Vinaya lineage lapsed. The Daibutsuden has been rebuilt twice after fire, once from around 1180 in Southern Song fashion under abbot Shunjōbō Chōgen and again in 1709; the present hall is roughly 30 percent smaller than its predecessor, reduced from eleven bays to seven for want of funds, while the late twelfth-century Nandaimon still shelters the 8.5-metre Niō carved by Unkei, Kaikei and their workshop. Today it is the Japanese headquarters of the Kegon school, its storehouse the Shōsōin holds Tenpyō-period treasures, and its Kon-dō, Nandaimon, Hokke-dō, Nigatsu-dō and other halls are National Treasures within the UNESCO Historic Monuments of Ancient Nara.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/T%C5%8Ddai-ji_Kon-d%C5%8D.jpg/960px-T%C5%8Ddai-ji_Kon-d%C5%8D.jpg","caption":"The Kon-dō (Daibutsuden, Great Buddha Hall) of Tōdai-ji, Nara. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Tōdai-ji","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/T%C5%8Ddai-ji"}]},
  {"name":"Kōfuku-ji","kind":"temple","sect":"Hossō","founded":"669","lat":34.685,"lon":135.8317,"note":"Kōfuku-ji began in 669 as a private vow by Kagami-no-Ōkimi, wife of Fujiwara no Kamatari, who prayed for her husband's recovery from illness; originally sited at Yamashina, it was moved to Fujiwara-kyō in 672 and dismantled a second time in 710 to be re-erected east of the new capital at Heijō-kyō. Kamatari's son Fujiwara no Fuhito named it after the Vimalakīrti Sūtra, and as the Fujiwara tutelary temple and national headquarters of the East Asian Yogācāra (Hossō) school it prospered with the family, retaining influence over the imperial government by aggressive means in some cases and keeping its standing among the Nanto Shichi Daiji even after rivals such as Tōdai-ji declined once the court moved to Heian-kyō. Empress Kōmyō, Fuhito's daughter and consort of Emperor Shōmu, ordered the five-storied pagoda in 730 and the Saikondō in 734, and the Fusō Ryakuki credits her with endowing Japan's first medical dispensary and welfare institution here. Civil war and fire wrecked the complex repeatedly; the Nandaimon, Chūmon, corridor and one of the three golden halls were never replaced, and the Central Golden Hall was only rebuilt in 2018. What remains is nonetheless exceptional: the Three-storied Pagoda of 1185, the Hoku'endō of 1210, the Tō-kondō of 1425 and the Five-storied Pagoda of 1426 are all National Treasures, as are the dry-lacquer Ashura among the Devas of the Eight Classes and the Ten Principal Disciples, and the temple forms part of the Historic Monuments of Ancient Nara.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Kofukuji12st5s3200.jpg/960px-Kofukuji12st5s3200.jpg","caption":"The Tō-kondō and Five-storied Pagoda of Kōfuku-ji, Nara. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Kōfuku-ji","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/K%C5%8Dfuku-ji"}]},
  {"name":"Enryaku-ji","kind":"monastery","sect":"Tendai","founded":"788","lat":35.0706,"lon":135.8411,"note":"Saichō (767 to 822), known posthumously as Dengyō Daishi, founded Enryaku-ji on Mount Hiei in 788 after bringing the Tendai teaching from China, and with Emperor Kanmu's support ordained a hundred disciples in 807 under a discipline that kept monks secluded on the mountain for twelve years of study and meditation before the ablest were retained or released into government service. The mountain became the seminary of medieval Japanese Buddhism, since the founders of Jōdo-shū, Jōdo Shinshū, Sōtō Zen and Nichiren Buddhism all spent time there. At its height the complex numbered as many as 3,000 sub-temples and an army of warrior monks (sōhei); a tenth-century succession quarrel between the lines of Ennin and Enchin split Tendai into the Mountain Order (sanmon) at Enryaku-ji and the Temple Order (jimon) at Mii-dera, after which the leadership hired mercenary armies that intimidated rivals and even marched on the capital to force monastic demands. Oda Nobunaga ended that militancy in 1571 by levelling the buildings and slaughtering the monks, and only the thirteenth-century Ruri-dō survived; the present structures date from the late sixteenth to the mid-seventeenth century, the main hall rebuilt in 1642 under Tokugawa Iemitsu, with some halls brought in from other temples, notably Mii-dera. The buildings cluster in three precincts, Tō-dō, Sai-tō and Yokokawa, and the monastery remains the Tendai headquarters, the centre of the kaihōgyō practice of the \"marathon monks\", and part of the UNESCO Historic Monuments of Ancient Kyoto.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Enryakuji_Konponchudo02s5s3200.jpg/960px-Enryakuji_Konponchudo02s5s3200.jpg","caption":"The Konpon Chū-dō of Enryaku-ji on Mount Hiei, Ōtsu. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Enryaku-ji","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enryaku-ji"}]},
  {"name":"Mii-dera (Onjō-ji)","kind":"temple","sect":"Tendai (Jimon branch)","founded":"672","lat":35.0269,"lon":135.8547,"note":"Founded in 672 at the foot of Mount Hiei near Lake Biwa, in the aftermath of the succession war that placed Emperor Tenmu on the throne, Nagara-san Onjō-ji was raised in memory of the emperor's brother, Tenji; the familiar name Mii-dera (\"Temple of Three Wells\") came nearly two centuries later from the abbot Enchin, after the springs used there for the ritual bathing of newborns. Under Enchin's leadership (859 to 891) it came to stand beside Tōdai-ji, Kōfuku-ji and Enryaku-ji as one of the four temples charged with protecting the capital, but his death opened a lasting rift within Tendai: Mii-dera became head of the Jimon branch against Enryaku-ji's Sanmon, a rivalry more geographic than doctrinal yet violent enough that Enryaku-ji's sōhei burned Mii-dera to the ground four times in the eleventh century alone. Its warrior-monks acted as political players in their own right, sheltering Prince Mochihito in June 1180 and marching with the Minamoto to the Byōdō-in, for which Taira no Kiyomori ordered the temple destroyed; much of the complex fell again to Oda Nobunaga's army in 1571. The present Kondō dates from 1599 and still houses a spring of sacred water, the Kannon-dō from 1072, and the Kōjō-in Kyakuden of 1601 is a National Treasure and a model of late Muromachi shoin-zukuri, decorated with fusuma by Kanō Sanraku. Some forty named buildings survive; the temple is station 14 on the Saigoku Kannon pilgrimage, and its bronze evening bell carries a body of legend, including a tale of the sōhei Benkei.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Miidera-no-bansho-M2075.jpg/960px-Miidera-no-bansho-M2075.jpg","caption":"The bronze evening bell (bansho) of Mii-dera hanging in its belfry, Ōtsu, Shiga Prefecture. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Mii-dera","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mii-dera"}]},
  {"name":"Kongōbu-ji","kind":"monastery","sect":"Shingon","founded":"819 (Kūkai's foundation on Mount Kōya); present building 1593","lat":34.2141,"lon":135.5841,"note":"Kongōbu-ji, the \"Temple of the Diamond Mountain Peak\", is the ecclesiastical head temple of Kōyasan Shingon Buddhism, seated on the high plateau of Mount Kōya that Kūkai settled in 819 and where the original monastery grew into the temple town of Kōya. The building a visitor enters today is not Kūkai's: it was raised in 1593 as Seigan-ji by Toyotomi Hideyoshi on the death of his mother, rebuilt in 1861, and given its present name only in 1869. Its interiors are hung with sliding doors (fusuma) painted by Kanō Tanyū (1602 to 1674) and members of the Kyoto Kanō school, while the modern Banryūtei is the largest rock garden in Japan at 2,340 square metres, its 140 granite stones set to suggest a pair of dragons rising from cloud to guard the temple. The temple remains a working seat of authority rather than a museum piece: its 414th abbot serves at the same time as archbishop of the Kōyasan Shingon school, and visitors may hear the monks' sermons and join sessions of ajikan, the Shingon practice of meditating on the letter A written in the Siddhaṃ script. Kōyasan, Kongōbu-ji included, was inscribed by UNESCO in 2004 within the \"Sacred Sites and Pilgrimage Routes in the Kii Mountain Range\".","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Kongobuji_Koyasan01n4272.jpg/960px-Kongobuji_Koyasan01n4272.jpg","caption":"Kongōbu-ji on Mount Kōya, Wakayama Prefecture. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Kongōbu-ji","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kongōbu-ji"}]},
  {"name":"Tōji","kind":"temple","sect":"Shingon","founded":"796","lat":34.98056,"lon":135.74778,"note":"Tō-ji, the \"East Temple\", was founded in 796, two years after the court moved to Heian-kyō, and was one of only three Buddhist temples permitted in the new capital; it alone survives, its partner Sai-ji having faded away in the sixteenth century through poor irrigation in Ukyō-ku and want of funds. Its formal name, Kyō-ō-gokoku-ji, states the purpose plainly: defence of the nation by means of the King of Doctrines, and that charge took esoteric form in 823, when Emperor Saga placed the temple in Kūkai's hands, from which time it has been a Shingon foundation with Yakushi Nyorai as its principal image. Tō-ji declined at the close of the Heian period and returned to prominence in the Kamakura period on the strength of Daishi shinkō, the cult of Kūkai himself, whose residence is commemorated by the Miedō. Five of its buildings are National Treasures in two categories: the Lotus Flower Gate (rengemon), the Miedō, the Kondō (which holds a Yakushi of 1603) and the five-storey pagoda, together with the Kanchi-in guest hall; the pagoda, at 54.8 metres the tallest wooden tower in Japan, was rebuilt in 1643 by order of the third Tokugawa shōgun, Iemitsu, after four earlier losses, and was itself designated a National Treasure in 1952. Tō-ji was inscribed by UNESCO in 1994 among the Historic Monuments of Ancient Kyoto, and on the twenty-first of each month, the day of Kūkai's death in 835, the grounds fill with the Kōbō-san market.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Toji_2015.JPG/960px-Toji_2015.JPG","caption":"The Tō-ji precinct in Minami-ku, Kyoto, with the five-storey pagoda (gojūnotō) rising behind the temple moat and wall. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Tō-ji","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tō-ji"}]},
  {"name":"Ise Grand Shrine","kind":"shrine","sect":"Shinto","founded":"4 BCE (traditional)","lat":34.453,"lon":136.7222,"note":"Ise Jingū, known simply as Jingū, is the holiest site in Shinto: a complex of some 125 shrines centred on the Naikū (Kōtai Jingū), dedicated to the sun goddess Amaterasu Ōmikami, and the Gekū (Toyouke Daijingū), dedicated to the deity of agriculture and food, Toyouke-hime. Tradition following the Nihon Shoki dates the Naikū to 4 BCE and ascribes its siting to Yamatohime-no-mikoto, daughter of Emperor Suinin, who wandered for twenty years from Mount Miwa through Ōmi and Mino until Amaterasu named Ise as the secluded land where she wished to dwell; third and fifth century datings have also been proposed, and the first shrine building at Naikū is credited to Emperor Tenmu, with the first ceremonial rebuilding carried out by Empress Jitō in 692. That rebuilding became the Shikinen Sengū, the twenty-year renewal expressing tokowaka, in which the shrines and the Uji Bridge are dismantled and rebuilt on an adjacent site by miyadaiku carpenters working without power tools; the present buildings, the 62nd iteration, date from 2013 and cost 57 billion yen (about US$550 million) raised from private donations alone, with the next renewal due in 2033. The architecture is yuitsu-shinmei-zukuri, a variant that may be used for no other shrine and that echoes ancient raised rice granaries: hinoki cypress joined without nails, pillars set directly into the ground, and a thatched reed roof carrying ten katsuogi and chigi cut flat on top to mark a female deity. Ise's standing was political as well as ritual, served from the late seventh century to the fourteenth by a Saiō, an imperial princess as high priestess, and drawing mass pilgrimage in the Edo period (okage mairi), when 3.62 million people are recorded as visiting in fifty days in 1625; the shrine is held to house the sacred mirror Yata no Kagami, and the public is still admitted no further than sight of the thatched roofs behind four tall tamagaki fences.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Naiku_04.jpg/960px-Naiku_04.jpg","caption":"The Naikū (Kōtai Jingū), inner shrine of Ise Jingū, in Ise, Mie Prefecture. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Ise Grand Shrine","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ise_Grand_Shrine"}]},
  {"name":"Izumo-taisha","kind":"shrine","sect":"Shinto","founded":"Ancient","lat":35.4019,"lon":132.6856,"note":"No record gives the date of Izumo-taisha's establishment; the Kojiki and Nihon Shoki account for it mythologically, as the dwelling Amaterasu presented to Ōkuninushi after he ceded his country to her grandson Ninigi-no-Mikoto, and the shrine is widely regarded as the oldest in Japan, predating Ise. Its main hall gives its name to taisha-zukuri, the oldest style of shrine building, with an off-centre entrance, nine support pillars dividing the interior into four sections and enormous scissor-shaped chigi on the roof; a record compiled around 950 describes a hall of roughly 48 metres, taller than the 45-metre hall of the Great Buddha at Tōdai-ji, and the discovery in 2000 of the remains of enormous pillars lends credence to that tradition. The structure was reduced in size around 1200, in the Kamakura period, and rebuilt in 1744 at its present 24 metres, by which time easier travel was turning Izumo into a central place of pilgrimage; renewal follows a sengū cycle of some 60 to 70 years, the deity having been moved for renovation in 1809, 1881 and 1953 and returned to the inner shrine before more than 8,000 people on 11 May 2013. Ōkuninushi is venerated as the deity of marriage and the god who establishes good relationships, and in the tenth lunar month the gods are believed to gather here to settle the coming year's marriages, deaths and births, so the Izumo region calls that month kamiarizuki (the month with gods) while the rest of Japan calls it kannazuki (the month without gods). The honden and attached buildings were designated National Treasures in 1952; visitors today pass the Kagura-den, hung with the largest shimenawa in Japan at 13.5 metres and about five tonnes, and the Shōkokan museum of treasures, while the Senge family, descendants of Amenohohi-no-mikoto who bore the title Izumo Kokusō, lost the administration when the shrine was ranked a Kanpei-taisha in 1871 and recovered it after the war, Takatoshi Senge becoming chief priest in 1947.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Izumo-taisha14bs4592.jpg/960px-Izumo-taisha14bs4592.jpg","caption":"The main hall of Izumo-taisha and its subsidiary halls behind the enclosing fence, showing the thatched gables and chigi finials of taisha-zukuri. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Izumo-taisha","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Izumo-taisha"}]},
  {"name":"Kasuga-taisha","kind":"shrine","sect":"Shinto","founded":"768","lat":34.6903,"lon":135.8456,"note":"Kasuga-taisha was established in 768 as the shrine of the Fujiwara family, its foundation legend telling how the first kami, Takemikazuchi, rode from Kashima Jingū on the back of a white deer to the summit of Mount Mikasa to protect Nara; imperial favour followed in the Nara period through Fujiwara influence and that of Empress Shōtoku, and from 1871 to 1946 the shrine ranked among the Kanpei-taisha, the first tier of government-supported shrines. Four kami are enshrined in four halls of identical form (Takemikazuchi, Futsunushi, Ame-no-Koyane and the consort Himegami), worshipped collectively as Kasuga Daimyōjin, a composite deity read through honji suijaku, the theory identifying Buddhist divinities with kami counterparts, which allowed a worshipper to address several gods at once. The sanctuary gave its name to the Kasuga-zukuri style, marked by sloping gabled roofs, katsuogi and chigi, and the complex, enclosed by four cloisters and holding a treasure house of hundreds of national treasures and other cultural properties (mostly Heian works, including gagaku drums and bronze mirrors), maintains shikinen zōtai, rebuilding to identical specifications every twenty years since the Nara period, the sixtieth renewal falling in 2016. Over three thousand stone and bronze lanterns line the approach and hang along the corridors; they are lit together only at Setsubun Mantōrō on 3 February and Chūgen Mantōrō on 14 and 15 August, while the auxiliary Wakamiya Jinja, founded in 1135, is the focus of the Kasuga Wakamiya festival each December. Hunting and logging have been forbidden on Kasugayama since 841, leaving the forest backdrop essentially unchanged since the Nara period, and the shrine together with the Kasugayama Primeval Forest is registered by UNESCO as part of the Historic Monuments of Ancient Nara, its freely roaming deer still regarded as sacred messengers of the gods.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Kasuga-taisha11bs3200.jpg/960px-Kasuga-taisha11bs3200.jpg","caption":"The vermilion gate and corridor of Kasuga-taisha in Nara, hung with bronze lanterns. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Kasuga-taisha","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kasuga-taisha"}]},
  {"name":"Itsukushima Shrine","kind":"shrine","sect":"Shinto","founded":"593","lat":34.2956,"lon":132.3186,"note":"Shrine tradition places the foundation in the first year of Empress Suiko (593), when the local chieftain Saeki Kuramoto is said to have raised a hall on the island for the three daughters of Susanoo (Ichikishimahime, Tagorihime and Tagitsuhime), the sanjoshin or three female deities of seas and storms; Itsukushima served as the ichinomiya, or chief shrine, of Aki Province. Its present form is owed to Taira no Kiyomori, who as governor of Aki contributed heavily to its construction from 1168 and ordered the main hall built in reverence for the tutelary god of navigation, making Miyajima the Taira clan shrine and a base for the family's maritime activity and its attempted monopoly of the Inland Sea trade with Song China; the Taira dedicated the celebrated Heike Nōkyō, thirty-two scrolls of the Lotus, Amida and Heart sutras copied by Kiyomori, his sons and other kinsmen and decorated with gold, silver and mother-of-pearl. Because the island itself was held to be a god and no births or deaths were allowed to pollute it, the shrine was built like a pier over the water in Shinden-zukuri style so that it appeared to float, separate from the land, and commoners had to steer their boats through the torii before approaching; the prohibition still stands, with no births or deaths permitted near the shrine since 1878. Later patrons continued the work: Mōri Motonari, who was said to have tainted the island's sacred ground by fighting Sue Takafusa there in 1555, rebuilt the honden in 1571, and a Noh stage extending from the haraiden dates to 1590, while the vermilion ōtorii of decay-resistant camphor wood, about 15 metres tall and given extra legs before and behind each pillar in the Ryōbu Shintō manner, stands in its present form from 1875 though a gate has occupied the spot since 1168. Typhoon Songda badly damaged the boardwalks and roof on 5 September 2004 and closed the shrine for repairs; it was inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1996, several of its buildings and possessions are designated National Treasures, and the Kangen-sai boat ritual initiated by Kiyomori is still performed each summer.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Itsukushima_Shrine_Torii_Gate_%2813890465459%29.jpg/960px-Itsukushima_Shrine_Torii_Gate_%2813890465459%29.jpg","caption":"The vermilion ōtorii of Itsukushima Shrine standing in the sea off Miyajima, Hiroshima Prefecture. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Itsukushima Shrine","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Itsukushima_Shrine"}]},
  {"name":"Kumano-Hongū Taisha","kind":"shrine","sect":"Shinto","founded":"Ancient","lat":33.84,"lon":135.7739,"note":"Kumano Hongū Taisha in Tanabe, Wakayama Prefecture, is the shrine to which all the ancient Kumano Kodō routes lead, and it enshrines the Kumano Sansho Gongen: Ketsumimiko, Hayatama and Fusubi. A pilgrim writing more than nine hundred years ago described a vast precinct of five main pavilions housing twelve deities, ringed by numerous lesser shrines and temples; fire and flood damaged it repeatedly over the centuries and it was always rebuilt to its original state, so faithfully that the reconstruction of 1803, following the last fire of 1776, is almost identical to a drawing of the grounds made some eight hundred years ago. The shrine stood until modern times at Ōyunohara, a sandbank at the confluence of the Kumano and Otonashi rivers, but a flood in 1889 partly destroyed it and the surviving buildings were moved to the present hillside site in 1891, where only three of the five pavilions were rebuilt; four deities were brought across and the remaining eight are still enshrined at Ōyunohara in two stone monuments. The halls are built of natural unfinished materials, roofed in hinoki cypress bark and jointed without nails, with bronze chigi and katsuogi crowning a thick roof that sweeps forward over the steps, and the sheltered corridor beneath the verandas, where pilgrims and ascetics once meditated, copied sutras, performed austerities and slept, and where Ippen Shōnin is said to have attained enlightenment, has earned the buildings their own designation as Kumano style, a mixture of the Kasuga and Taisha forms. Since 2000 the entrance to the old sandbank has been marked by the Ōtorii, at 33.9 metres high and 42 metres wide the largest torii gate in the world, made of steel weighing 172 tonnes; the shrine is included with the rest of the Kumano Sanzan in the World Heritage Site Sacred Sites and Pilgrimage Routes in the Kii Mountain Range, and the three-legged crow Yatagarasu is honoured at the Yata-no-Hi fire festival held at Ōyunohara on the last Saturday of August.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/90/Shrine_Kumano_hongu_torii01.jpg","caption":"The torii at the approach to Kumano Hongū Taisha, beside a banner bearing the Yatagarasu three-legged crow. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Kumano Hongū Taisha","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kumano_Hong%C5%AB_Taisha"}]},
  {"name":"Kinkaku-ji (Temple of the Golden Pavilion)","kind":"temple","sect":"Rinzai Zen","founded":"1397","lat":35.0393,"lon":135.7294,"note":"The site began as Kitayama-dai, the villa of the courtier Saionji Kintsune, until the shōgun Ashikaga Yoshimitsu bought it from the Saionji family in 1397 and remade it as his Kitayama retirement estate; on his death it was converted, according to his own wishes, into a Zen temple officially named Rokuon-ji. The pavilion itself, believed to have been built in 1399, is a shariden housing relics of the Buddha, and it stacks three architectural idioms one above another: the Heian aristocratic shinden-zukuri of the ground floor (Hō-sui-in), the warrior buke-zukuri of the second, which holds a Buddha hall and a shrine to Kannon (Chō-on-dō), and Chinese-derived Zen zenshū-butsuden-zukuri at the top (Kukkyō-chō), the upper two storeys sheathed in gold leaf beneath a bronze hōō finial. It sits in a kaiyū-shiki strolling garden laid out after descriptions of Amida's Western Paradise, its reflection carried on the Kyōko-chi or Mirror Pond, borrowing the surrounding scenery (shakkei) in the manner that makes the Muromachi period a classical age of Japanese garden design. Every building in the complex except the pavilion burned during the Ōnin War (1467 to 1477), and the pavilion in turn was destroyed on 2 July 1950 by a twenty-two-year-old novice, Hayashi Shōken, an episode fictionalised in Mishima Yukio's novel of 1956. What stands today is the reconstruction of 1955, regilded in 1986 and 1987 with gold leaf far thicker than the original; the temple is a National Special Historic Site and one of the seventeen Historic Monuments of Ancient Kyoto inscribed by UNESCO.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Golden_Pavilion_Kinkaku-ji_water_mirror_2024.jpg/960px-Golden_Pavilion_Kinkaku-ji_water_mirror_2024.jpg","caption":"The Golden Pavilion (Kinkaku) at Rokuon-ji, Kyoto, reflected in the Kyōko-chi pond. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Kinkaku-ji","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kinkaku-ji"}]},
  {"name":"Ginkaku-ji (Temple of the Silver Pavilion)","kind":"temple","sect":"Rinzai Zen","founded":"1482","lat":35.0267,"lon":135.7983,"note":"Ashikaga Yoshimasa was planning a retirement villa at the foot of the Higashiyama hills as early as 1460, furnishing it with stones and pines carried off from the Flowery Palace and the Muromachi Hall after civil war had wrecked them, and construction of its two-storeyed Kannon-den began on 21 February 1482. He meant the building to answer the golden pavilion raised by his grandfather Ashikaga Yoshimitsu and intended to sheathe it in silver foil, but the Ōnin War halted the work and the covering was never applied; the nickname Ginkaku, Silver Pavilion, is in fact no older than the Edo period, and the unfinished surface is now read as an emblem of wabi-sabi. Yoshimasa took Buddhist vows in 1485 and is said to have sat in the pavilion contemplating the gardens while Kyoto burned, and on his death on 27 January 1490 the villa became a Zen temple named Jishō-ji after his Buddhist name; it belongs today to the Shōkoku-ji branch of Rinzai Zen. The complex is one of the defining monuments of Higashiyama bunka, the cultural flowering that Yoshimasa's patronage set in motion, although its garden was financed by the shogunate as a retreat for aesthetic enjoyment rather than laid out by monks for zazen. Visitors now walk wooded, deeply mossed grounds and a garden traditionally attributed to Sōami, with a sand garden whose carefully formed cone is said to stand for Mount Fuji; the restoration begun in February 2008 deliberately left the silver foil unapplied and the lacquer unrenewed.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Ginkakuji_Kyoto03-r.jpg/960px-Ginkakuji_Kyoto03-r.jpg","caption":"The Kannon-den, or Silver Pavilion, at Jishō-ji (Ginkaku-ji), Kyoto. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Ginkaku-ji","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ginkaku-ji"}]},
  {"name":"Tenryū-ji","kind":"temple","sect":"Rinzai Zen","founded":"1339","lat":35.0153,"lon":135.6778,"note":"The ground was religious long before the temple: Empress Tachibana no Kachiko, wife of Emperor Saga, founded Danrin-ji here in the early Heian period, and it was here that the monk Gikū taught Zen in Japan for the first time, though his teaching found little welcome and the site fell into four centuries of decay before Emperor Go-Saga and his son Emperor Kameyama rebuilt it in the mid-thirteenth century as the Kameyama Detached Palace. Ashikaga Takauji had the palace converted into a temple in 1339 to hold memorial services for Emperor Go-Daigo, the former friend turned enemy who had decreed his execution after the failed Kenmu Restoration, and he had the Zen monk Musō Soseki build it and serve as its first chief priest; the intended name Ryakuō Shiseizen-ji was set aside after Takauji's younger brother Tadayoshi dreamed of a golden dragon over the Ōi River, and the finished temple was dedicated in 1345 at a ceremony that doubled as Go-Daigo's memorial. Its finances were commercial as much as devotional: two trading vessels, the Tenryūji-bune, were launched in 1342 to raise building funds, and in the 1430s the temple entered a tributary relationship with Ming China under which formal trade could proceed in exchange for Chinese control over the succession of its abbots, giving Tenryū-ji something near a monopoly on Japan's legitimate China trade, a circuit its priests helped coordinate between China, Okinawa and Japan into the nineteenth century. Ranked first among Kyoto's Five Mountains (Gozan), it grew to roughly 330,000 square metres and is said once to have held some 150 sub-temples, but it burned six times in the medieval period (1358, 1367, 1373, 1380, 1447 and 1467), was destroyed again in the Ōnin War, lost to fire once more in 1815 and severely damaged in the Kinmon Incident of 1864, so that almost every building now standing is a late Meiji reconstruction. The Momoyama-style Chokushi gate is the oldest structure on the grounds, a cloud dragon (Unryū-zu) by Suzuki Shōnen covers the ceiling of the lecture hall rebuilt in 1900, and Musō Soseki's garden circling Sōgen Pond, a Special Place of Scenic Beauty, preserves only traces of its original design; the temple was inscribed by UNESCO in 1994 among the Historic Monuments of Ancient Kyoto.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Tenryuji_Kyoto.jpg/960px-Tenryuji_Kyoto.jpg","caption":"Tenryū-ji in Ukyō Ward, Kyoto, head temple of the Tenryū-ji branch of Rinzai Zen. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Tenryū-ji","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tenry%C5%AB-ji"}]},
  {"name":"Shōkoku-ji","kind":"temple","sect":"Rinzai Zen","founded":"1382","lat":35.0372,"lon":135.7417,"note":"Shōkoku-ji, formally Mannen-zan Shōkoku Shōten Zenji, was founded in 1382 by the shōgun Ashikaga Yoshimitsu; work on the central halls began in 1383 and the complex was dedicated in 1392, marked by a banquet for the great officers of the imperial court and the military leaders of the day whose pomp was said to equal an imperial occasion. Yoshimitsu designated the Zen master Shun'oku Myōha founding abbot in 1383, but Myōha insisted that the honour be accorded posthumously to his own teacher Musō Soseki, a decision formally proclaimed in 1385. Fire destroyed the entire complex in 1394 and Yoshimitsu financed its rebuilding, the first of many reconstructions, notably during the Ōnin War; later patrons included Toyotomi Hideyoshi, Toyotomi Hideyori and Tokugawa Ieyasu, with Hideyori paying for the 1605 rebuilding of the Hattō (Dharma Hall), Ieyasu donating the Sanmon in 1609, and Emperor Go-Mizunoo giving an imperial palace building to serve as the Kaisandō, the Founder's Hall. The conflagration of 1788 devastated the complex once more but spared that seventeenth-century Hattō, which survives as an Important Cultural Property and the oldest building of its type in Japan; its slightly domed ceiling carries a dragon by Kanō Mitsunobu, symbol of the rain of Buddhist teachings, and a handclap on the stone floor below returns as a rolling echo. Ranked second among the Kyoto Gozan, and briefly first in 1392, Shōkoku-ji is today the headquarters of one of the fourteen autonomous branches of Rinzai Zen, with more than ninety affiliated temples including the Golden and Silver Pavilions, and it houses the Jōtenkaku Museum on its grounds.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/150815_Shokokuji_Kyoto_Japan02s3.jpg/960px-150815_Shokokuji_Kyoto_Japan02s3.jpg","caption":"Shōkoku-ji, northern Kyoto, headquarters of the Shōkoku-ji branch of Rinzai Zen. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Shōkoku-ji","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sh%C5%8Dkoku-ji"}]},
  {"name":"Nanzen-ji","kind":"temple","sect":"Rinzai Zen","founded":"1291","lat":35.0089,"lon":135.7919,"note":"Emperor Kameyama founded Nanzen-ji, formerly Zenrin-ji, in 1291 on the site of his own detached palace in the eastern hills of Kyoto, and it remains the headquarters of the Nanzen-ji branch of Rinzai Zen. It was never counted among the five great Zen temples of the Kyoto Gozan, yet it presided over them: once Ashikaga Yoshimitsu completed Shōkoku-ji in 1386 the ranking was revised to set Nanzen-ji above the whole system in a class of its own, titled First Temple of the Land and charged with a supervising role over the network. Fire took the complex in 1393, 1447 and 1467; it was rebuilt in 1597 and enlarged in the Edo period, its sub-temples fluctuating over time between nine and twelve. The Sanmon a visitor passes today was first raised in the 13th century, pulled down by government order in 1369 and rebuilt in 1628, and its elevated gallery supplied the setting for the thief Ishikawa Goemon's celebrated speech in the kabuki play Sanmon Gosan no Kiri of 1778. Behind it stands the hōjō or abbot's quarters, a National Treasure presented by Emperor Go-Yōzei and hung with Kanō Tan'yū's gold-ground tigers, fronted by Kobori Enshū's karesansui garden of the early 1600s (a designated Place of Scenic Beauty), while a brick aqueduct of 1890 carries Lake Biwa Canal water straight through the precinct.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/251213_Nanzen-ji_Kyoto_Japan01s3.jpg/960px-251213_Nanzen-ji_Kyoto_Japan01s3.jpg","caption":"Nanzen-ji in Kyoto. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Nanzen-ji","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nanzen-ji"}]},
  {"name":"Kenchō-ji","kind":"temple","sect":"Rinzai Zen","founded":"1253","lat":35.3256,"lon":139.5553,"note":"Kenchō-ji was completed in 1253, the fifth year of the Kenchō era from which it takes its name, built on the orders of Emperor Go-Fukakusa and at the invitation of the regent Hōjō Tokiyori, who summoned the Song Chinese master Lanxi Daolong (Rankei Dōryū) to lead it; it is the oldest Zen training monastery in Japan and ranks first among the Kamakura Gozan. Tokiyori's patronage was devotional and political at once, for the Five Mountain network that Kenchō-ji headed operated as a de facto ministry, circulating shogunal law, monitoring provincial conditions and supplying the Hōjō and later the Ashikaga with translators, diplomats and advisers behind a religious mask. Under that patronage the Gozan temples became centres of learning and produced the Literature of the Five Mountains, whose scholars reached far into national politics before the system decayed along with the Ashikaga shogunate; Kenchō-ji's own revival waited until the 19th century and the Zen master Aozora Kandō. Most of the original shichidō garan and its forty-nine subtemples burned in the fires of the 14th and 15th centuries, leaving ten subtemples today, though the surviving halls still stand in the classic Zen alignment from north to south. A visitor moves past the Sanmon of 1754, nicknamed Tanuki-mon after the raccoon dog said to have raised its funds disguised as a monk, the National Treasure bell cast in 1255, the Butsuden and Karamon brought from Zōjō-ji in 1647, and the Hattō of 1814, the largest wooden Buddhist structure in eastern Japan, before reaching the Shin-ji Ike pond garden attributed to Musō Soseki and the hillside shrine of Hansōbō, brought here in 1890.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Kenchoji_Sanmon_2009.jpg/960px-Kenchoji_Sanmon_2009.jpg","caption":"The Sanmon gate of Kenchō-ji, Kamakura. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Kenchō-ji","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kench%C5%8D-ji"}]},
  {"name":"Engaku-ji","kind":"temple","sect":"Rinzai Zen","founded":"1282","lat":35.3289,"lon":139.5528,"note":"Engaku-ji was founded in 1282 by the Chinese master Mugaku Sōgen (1226 to 1286) at the request of the Kamakura regent Hōjō Tokimune, a long-committed patron of Zen who had just repelled the Mongol invasions of 1274 and 1281 and wanted a temple to honour the dead of both sides while serving as a centre from which Zen could spread. Its name comes from a copy of the Sutra of Perfect Enlightenment (engaku-kyō) said to have been dug from the hillside in a stone chest as building began. Ranked second among the Kamakura Gozan, the complex keeps the classical Zen monastic plan, its eighteen temples climbing a wooded slope with the major halls set in a straight perpendicular line; its present form is largely the work of the priest Seisetsu Shucho (1745 to 1820), who rebuilt and consolidated the site around the 500th anniversary of the founding in 1785. Two National Treasures anchor the precinct: the Shariden, a reliquary hall in karayō or Zenshū-yō style close to Song models, moved here from the Taiheiji convent after the original of 1285 burned in 1563 and said to hold a tooth of Shakyamuni presented to Minamoto no Sanetomo, and the Great Bell (Ōgane) cast in 1301 by Mononobe Kunimitsu at the order of Hōjō Sadatoki, at 2.6 metres the largest in the Kantō. In the Meiji era Engaku-ji became the chief centre of Zen instruction in the Kantō under the abbots Imakita Kōsen and Sōyen Shaku, who taught Daisetz Teitaro Suzuki; Tokimune himself is buried at the thatched Butsunichian, and zazen is still practised publicly in the Butsuden and Kojirin.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Stairs_to_Sanmon%2C_Engaku-ji.jpg/960px-Stairs_to_Sanmon%2C_Engaku-ji.jpg","caption":"The stairs up to the Sanmon at Engaku-ji, Kamakura. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Engaku-ji","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Engaku-ji"}]},
  {"name":"Negoro-ji","kind":"temple","sect":"Shingi Shingon (New Shingon)","founded":"1132 (Negoro estate granted to Kakuban; the lineage moved here in 1288)","lat":34.2872,"lon":135.3167,"note":"Negoro-ji began as an estate in Kii Province granted in 1132 by the retired Emperor Toba to the reformer Kakuban, who from 1131 had headed Shingon on Mount Kōya and made enemies there by trying to reunite the Ōno and Hirosawa branches, to assert Kōya's authority over the metropolitan headquarters at Tō-ji, and to fold Pure Land elements into Shingon orthodoxy through a new rite, the himitsu nembutsu or esoteric nembutsu. He resigned his posts in 1135, and after armed followers of rival factions burned his residence in 1140 he fled south to this mountain, dying here in 1143; his disciple Raiyū (1226 to 1304) moved the Daidenbō-in and Mitsugon-in chapels down from Kōya in 1288 and made Negoro-ji the seat of an independent school, Shingi Shingon or New Shingon. By the late Muromachi period the mountainside had swollen into a religious city of roughly 450 temples and chapels (2,700 by some accounts), controlling estates assessed at 720,000 koku and fielding some 10,000 Negoro-shū soldiers, many carrying the newly introduced matchlock, which made the temple the equal of a major Sengoku daimyō in economic and military might. It had assisted Oda Nobunaga during the Ishiyama Hongan-ji War of 1570 to 1580 and Tokugawa Ieyasu at Komaki and Nagakute in 1584, but it earned the enmity of Toyotomi Hideyoshi, whose invasion of Kii in 1585 left most of the complex in ashes; because Negoro-ji surrendered without resistance, whether the fire was set on Hideyoshi's orders, by the attackers, or by the defenders remains disputed. Rebuilding proceeded under Tokugawa patronage, Ieyasu donating the buildings of Hideyoshi's Shoun-ji after 1615 and the Kishū Tokugawa sustaining the work through the Edo period, so that the 40 metre Daitō, the largest tahōtō pagoda in Japan (begun around 1480, finished around 1547, still pitted with bullet holes from the 1585 attack and a National Treasure since 1899), now stands beside the Daishi-dō of about 1391 on grounds designated a National Historic Site and Place of Scenic Beauty in 2007.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Negoroji01s3200.jpg/960px-Negoroji01s3200.jpg","caption":"Negoro-ji in Iwade, Wakayama Prefecture. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Negoro-ji","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Negoro-ji"}]},
  {"name":"Ishiyama Hongan-ji","kind":"temple","sect":"Jōdo Shinshū","founded":"1496","lat":34.6676,"lon":135.5064,"note":"Founded in 1496, when Rennyo, the great revivalist abbot of Jōdo Shinshū (then commonly called Ikkō-shū), retired to a long slope known as Ōzaka at the mouth of the Yodo River in Settsu Province, on ground that recent archaeology places atop the ruins of the ancient Naniwa imperial palace. His small devotional hall drew followers so quickly that the temple and its surrounding town had reached roughly their final extent by his death three years later, and after the destruction of the Yamashina Mido in 1532 it served as the head temple of the Ikkō sect and the principal fortress of the Ikkō-ikki, the leagues of warrior priests and commoners who opposed samurai rule. Its coastal position made it near impregnable: about a hundred priests patrolled at any given hour, upwards of ten thousand could be summoned to battle by the ringing of a bell, contributions arrived through a system of brokers based in the merchant port of Sakai, and the Mōri clan resupplied the fortress when it was under siege. Oda Nobunaga invested it in 1570 and was held off for eleven years, the longest siege in Japanese history, until Abbot Kōsa (Kennyo) was persuaded to surrender in August 1580, whereupon the entire complex was set aflame, by some accounts from within, to deny Nobunaga any material gain from his victory. Nothing of it remains to visit: three years later Toyotomi Hideyoshi began raising Osaka Castle on the same site.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/%E5%A4%A7%E5%9D%82%E6%9C%AC%E9%A1%98%E5%AF%BA.jpg/960px-%E5%A4%A7%E5%9D%82%E6%9C%AC%E9%A1%98%E5%AF%BA.jpg","caption":"A scale model of the Ishiyama Hongan-ji fortress-temple at Osaka. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Ishiyama Hongan-ji","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ishiyama_Hongan-ji"}]},
  {"name":"Nishi Hongan-ji","kind":"temple","sect":"Jōdo Shinshū","founded":"1591","lat":34.9933,"lon":135.7408,"note":"The Hongan-ji line began in 1321 at the Ōtani Mausoleum, burial place of Shinran, founder of Jōdo Shinshū, whose great-grandson Kakunyo became its first chief priest and third monshu and dedicated it to the worship of Amida; the present Kyoto compound dates from 1591, when Toyotomi Hideyoshi granted Kennyo this land in reward for his surrender at Ishiyama Hongan-ji. Kennyo's son Kyōnyo, publicly disowned for refusing that surrender, threw his support behind Tokugawa Ieyasu instead and was rewarded with a temple immediately to the east, known from 1603 as Higashi Hongan-ji; in 1619 the government recognised the two as separate congregations, and Nishi Hongan-ji has remained head temple of the Hongwanji-ha. Seven of its structures are designated National Treasures, among them the Goeidō or Founder's Hall of 1636, rebuilt after an earthquake in 1596 and a fire in 1617, which enshrines a wooden image of Shinran and hosts the temple's major ceremonies, and the Amidadō of 1760, the main worship hall, whose altar holds an Amida flanked by portraits of six of the seven Pure Land masters together with images of Shinran's master Hōnen and of Prince Shōtoku. The karamon gate, built for Hideyoshi's Fushimi Castle around 1598 and moved here in 1632 for a planned visit by Tokugawa Iemitsu, is carved so densely with flowers, animals and figures from Chinese legend that it is nicknamed the Higurashi no Mon, the 'all day gate'. Much of the complex survives from the Azuchi-Momoyama and early Edo periods, and in 1994 Nishi Hongan-ji was inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List as part of the Historic Monuments of Ancient Kyoto.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/170216_Nishi_Honganji_Kyoto_Japan03s4.jpg/960px-170216_Nishi_Honganji_Kyoto_Japan03s4.jpg","caption":"The Amidadō and Goeidō of Nishi Hongan-ji, Kyoto, both National Treasures. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Nishi Hongan-ji","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nishi_Hongan-ji"}]},
  {"name":"Higashi Hongan-ji","kind":"temple","sect":"Jōdo Shinshū","founded":"1602","lat":34.9953,"lon":135.7522,"note":"Higashi Hongan-ji, the Eastern Monastery of the Original Vow, was established in 1602 when Tokugawa Ieyasu divided Jōdo Shinshū in two in order to diminish its power, and it has been the head temple of the Ōtani-ha branch ever since, though the buildings first stood on their present ground only in 1658. Its precinct holds the mausoleum of Shinran, founder of the sect, first constructed in 1272 and relocated several times before reaching this site in 1670. The main halls burned four times during the Edo period: in the Great Tenmei Fire of 1788 (rebuilt 1797), an accidental fire in 1823 (rebuilt 1835), a third in 1858 whose temporary replacements were run up in time for Shinran's 600th memorial service in 1861, and finally the city-wide conflagration caused by the Kinmon incident of 19 July 1864. The Tokugawa shogunate had repeatedly funded the rebuilding, but after 1864 reconstruction waited until 1879, once the fall of the shogunate and the upheaval of the Meiji Restoration had settled; the Goeidō and Amida Hall were completed in 1895 and the remaining buildings by 1911, and these are the halls a visitor sees today. The Goeidō, enshrining an image of Shinran, is among the largest wooden structures in the world at 76 metres long, 58 metres wide and 38 metres high, while the Amida Hall beside it, ornately worked in gold leaf and Meiji-era art, holds images of Amida and of Prince Shōtoku; Kyoto knows the two rival compounds affectionately as Ohigashisan and Onissan, Honorable Mr. East and Honorable Mr. West.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/170216_Higashi_Honganji_Kyoto_Japan03bs5.jpg/960px-170216_Higashi_Honganji_Kyoto_Japan03bs5.jpg","caption":"Higashi Hongan-ji, Kyoto, head temple of the Jōdo Shinshū Ōtani-ha. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Higashi Hongan-ji","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Higashi_Hongan-ji"}]},
  {"name":"Byōdō-in","kind":"temple","sect":"Jōdo/Tendai","founded":"1052","lat":34.8844,"lon":135.8081,"note":"Byōdō-in, the 'Temple of Equality', began as a country villa built at Uji in 998 for the high-ranking courtier Minamoto no Shigenobu, Minister of the Left; Fujiwara no Michinaga bought the property from the courtier's widow, and Michinaga's son Fujiwara no Yorimichi converted it into a Buddhist temple in 1052. That year was widely believed to mark the first of mappō, the degenerate Latter Day of the Law, and the anxiety it produced turned aristocrats and monks toward the Pure Land of Amida, the devotion the new temple was built to serve. The Amida-dō, completed in 1053 and known from the early Edo period as the Hōō-dō or Phoenix Hall (both for the pair of Chinese phoenixes on its roof and for its outspread wing and tail corridors), stands at the edge of an artificial pond in a Jōdo-shiki garden, the hall facing east so that a worshipper crosses the water toward it as toward the Pure Land itself; within sits Jōchō's gold-leafed cypress Amida, carved in the yosegi technique of hollowed blocks joined from the inside, whose proportions he made canonical. Kusunoki Masashige set fire to the precinct in 1336 while fighting the Ashikaga and the Phoenix Hall is the only original building to survive; from the seventeenth century two sub-temples, Jōdo-in of the Jōdo sect and Saishō-in of the Tendai sect, cooperated to keep the place running, and Byōdō-in remains jointly a Jōdo-shū and Tendai-shū temple. The hall, its Amida, the temple bell, the twenty-six praying bodhisattvas on clouds and the raigō paintings on the doors are all National Treasures, most now displayed in the largely subterranean Hōshōkan Museum opened in 2001; the site was inscribed by UNESCO in 1994 as part of the Historic Monuments of Ancient Kyoto, and the hall appears on the ten yen coin, its phoenix on the ten thousand yen note.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Phoenix_Hall%2C_Byodo-in%2C_November_2016_-01.jpg/960px-Phoenix_Hall%2C_Byodo-in%2C_November_2016_-01.jpg","caption":"The Phoenix Hall (Hōō-dō) of Byōdō-in at Uji. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Byōdō-in","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/By%C5%8Dd%C5%8D-in"}]},
  {"name":"Zenkō-ji","kind":"temple","sect":"Tendai/Jōdo","founded":"642","lat":36.6505,"lon":138.1917,"note":"Zenkō-ji was founded in the seventh century, before Japanese Buddhism divided into sects, and the town that grew around it became the modern city of Nagano; it is still co-managed by twenty-five priests of the Tendai school and fourteen of the Jōdo-shū, seated respectively at the Daikanjin, residence of the chief priest, and the Daihongan, which houses the nunnery and the high priestess. Its honzon is an image of Amida said to have reached Japan from India by way of the Korean peninsula in the sixth century, in the reign of Emperor Kinmei, and rumoured to be the first Buddhist statue brought to the country; legend holds that the image, having set two clans against one another, was thrown into a canal and recovered by Honda Yoshimitsu, whose name read in the Chinese manner gave the temple its own. That icon is a hibutsu (hidden Buddha) which the temple's commandments forbid anyone to view, the chief priest included, so a substitute image, the Maedachi Honzon, is shown once every six years at the Gokaichō (the most recent showing, put off from 2021 to 2022 by the pandemic, was extended to eighty-eight days to thin the crowds). Copies of the icon proliferated from the end of the Kamakura period, producing \"Shin-Zenkō-ji\" foundations across the provinces, and in the sixteenth century the temple lay between Uesugi Kenshin, who used it as a base of operations, and Takeda Shingen; an anxious chief abbot founded a replacement Zenkō-ji at what is now Kōfu, and in 1598 Toyotomi Hideyoshi moved the hibutsu to Kyoto and then to Shinano before it returned to Nagano. Visitors pass the Niōmon with its guardian Niō, then the Sanmon and the Kyōzō of 1759, whose octagonal sūtra case of 1694 is turned for merit (both are Important Cultural Properties), to reach the Hon-dō, a National Treasure, below which a wholly dark corridor invites pilgrims to feel for the key to Amida's Western Paradise.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/160501_Zenkoji_Nagano_Japan06s3.jpg/960px-160501_Zenkoji_Nagano_Japan06s3.jpg","caption":"The Hon-dō (main hall) of Zenkō-ji in Nagano, a National Treasure. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Zenkō-ji","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zenk%C5%8D-ji"}]},
  {"name":"Kiyomizu-dera","kind":"temple","sect":"Hossō (today Kita-Hossō)","founded":"778","lat":34.9948,"lon":135.7851,"note":"Kiyomizu-dera, properly Otowa-san Kiyomizu-dera, stands on Mount Otowa in the Higashiyama hills and is one of the very few Kyoto temples older than Heian-kyō itself. Its founding legend, preserved in the Kiyomizu-dera Engi and retold in the Konjaku Monogatarishū, has the Kōfuku-ji monk Kenshin (later Enchin) follow a golden stream in 778 to the ascetic Gyōei, from whose sacred tree he carved a Senjū Kannon; two years later the warrior Sakanoue no Tamuramaro, hunting deer on the mountain for his sick wife, was turned to Kannon worship, gave his own residence as the main hall and later prayed here before campaigning against the Emishi. Granted temple land in 805 and imperial recognition as Kita Kannon-ji in 810, it long belonged to the Hossō establishment of Kōfuku-ji and was dragged into that temple's feud with Enryaku-ji, whose warrior monks burned it in 1165; it burned again in the Ōnin War in 1469, when the Ji-sect fundraiser Gan'ami organised the rebuilding, and in all the complex burned nine times before the fire of 1629. The present Hon-dō, a National Treasure, was rebuilt in 1633 with a donation from Tokugawa Iemitsu, and its broad kakezukuri veranda, thrust out over the slope on tall pillars and built without a single nail, gave Japanese the phrase about \"jumping off the stage at Kiyomizu\" (234 Edo-period jumps were recorded, 85.4 per cent of them survived, and the practice was banned in 1872). Fifteen further structures are Important Cultural Properties, the Otowa waterfall below the hall still sends down three channels of wish-granting water, and the temple, sixteenth stop on the Saigoku Kannon pilgrimage and a Kita-Hossō foundation since its abbot Ōnishi Ryokei broke from the Hossō sect in 1965, lies within the Historic Monuments of Ancient Kyoto World Heritage Site.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Kiyomizu.jpg/960px-Kiyomizu.jpg","caption":"The Hon-dō of Kiyomizu-dera in Kyoto, its kakezukuri veranda carried on tall pillars above the Higashiyama slope. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Kiyomizu-dera","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kiyomizu-dera"}]},
  {"name":"Sensō-ji","kind":"temple","sect":"Tendai (independent since 1950)","founded":"645","lat":35.7148,"lon":139.7963,"note":"Sensō-ji, formally Kinryū-zan Sensō-ji and popularly Asakusa Kannon, is Tokyo's oldest-established temple and is dedicated to Kannon, the bodhisattva of compassion. Legend recorded in the Sensō Engi has the brothers Hinokuma Hamanari and Takenari net a small golden Kannon from the Sumida River in 628, after which their village headman Haji no Nakatomo took vows and turned his house into a temple; in 645 the monk Katsumi rebuilt it and, following a dream, declared the image a hibutsu, which has never since been shown. The temple enters documented history in the Azuma kagami, which records Minamoto no Yoritomo summoning Asakusa carpenters in 1181 to build Tsurugaoka Hachiman-gū and Sensō-ji monks attending Emperor Go-Shirakawa's memorial service in 1192; in 1590 Tokugawa Ieyasu made it an official prayer temple of his shogunate with 500 koku of land, and after fires in 1631 and 1642 Tokugawa Iemitsu paid for a new five-story pagoda in 1648 and main hall in 1649. Under that patronage Asakusa became the pleasure ground of commoner Edo: shops that became the Nakamise were licensed along the approach in 1685 in exchange for cleaning the precinct, street performances filled the \"Okuyama\" ground behind the hall, and the three licensed Edo theatres moved to the district in 1843 and 1844. Saved from the 1923 Great Kantō earthquake by a bucket brigade, the main hall (a national treasure at the time) and the pagoda were destroyed in the firebombing of 10 March 1945; the hall was rebuilt in 1951 to 1958 and now carries a titanium-tiled roof, the Kaminarimon followed in 1960 with money from Konosuke Matsushita, the Hōzōmon in 1964 and the pagoda in 1973, and the temple, which left the Tendai sect to become independent in 1950, still owns the Heian-period Lotus Sutra set designated a National Treasure.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Sensoji_2023.jpg/960px-Sensoji_2023.jpg","caption":"The main hall of Sensō-ji at Asakusa, Tokyo, rebuilt after the 1945 firebombing. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Sensō-ji","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sens%C5%8D-ji"}]},
  {"name":"Rinnō-ji","kind":"temple","sect":"Tendai","founded":"766","lat":36.7456,"lon":139.6089,"note":"Rinnō-ji is the Tendai temple at the heart of Nikkō, established in 766 by the monk Shōdō Shōnin (735 to 817); its isolation deep in the mountains soon drew other monks in search of solitude, and it remains an important base for ascetic training among Tendai monks. Its best-known building, the Sanbutsudō or Three Buddha Hall, enshrines gold-leafed images of Amida, Senju Kannon and Batō Kannon, which are understood as the Buddhist manifestations of Nikkō's three mountain kami worshipped next door at Futarasan Shrine. The temple also administers the Taiyū-in Reibyō, the mausoleum of the third shōgun Tokugawa Iemitsu (1604 to 1651), built in 1653 in gongen-zukuri style; technically a Shinto shrine, it is a designated National Treasure and was praised by the World Heritage Committee as a masterpiece of architecture and decoration. Beside the Sanbutsudō lie the Shōyō-en garden and the Homotsu-den treasure house, which displays about fifty items at a time from a collection of sculpture, painting, calligraphy and scrolls running back to the eighth century, while the temple also holds the Daihatsu nehankyō shūge, a National Treasure commentary on the Nirvana Sutra in fifty-nine handscrolls of Nara and Heian date. Its hibutsu Chinjō Yasha, a yakṣa taken as a manifestation of Vaiśravaṇa and a protector of the state, is unveiled in the Great Goma Hall only once every nine years (most recently on 3 February 2023), and Rinnō-ji, with thirty-seven of its structures designated Important Cultural Properties, forms part of the Shrines and Temples of Nikkō World Heritage Site together with Nikkō Tōshō-gū and Futarasan Shrine.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/NikkoRinnoji5390.jpg/960px-NikkoRinnoji5390.jpg","caption":"The vermilion Sanbutsudō (Three Buddha Hall) of Rinnō-ji at Nikkō. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Rinnō-ji","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rinn%C5%8D-ji"}]},
  {"name":"Fushimi Inari Taisha","kind":"shrine","sect":"Shinto","founded":"711","lat":34.9671,"lon":135.7727,"note":"Fushimi Inari-taisha, the head shrine of the kami Inari, was formally founded in 711 (the Wadō era) by the Hata clan, toraijin immigrants from the Korean Peninsula who had settled Yamashiro Province during the Kofun period and brought continental skills in irrigation, sericulture and sake brewing; the Yamashiro-no-kuni Fudoki attributes its establishment to Hata no Irogu, who is said to have shot an arrow at a rice cake that turned into a white bird and alighted on the peak of Mount Inari. Inari remains above all the kami of rice and agriculture, but the clan's technical expertise and its close ties to the imperial court carried the deity from a private clan tutelary (ujigami) into a national protector of the harvest and of the state, and merchants in time took Inari as the patron of business. The shrine drew imperial patronage in the early Heian period and was among the sixteen shrines to receive Emperor Murakami's heihaku offerings in 965; from 1871 to 1946 it ranked as a Kanpei-taisha, in the first class of government-supported shrines, and it is said today to have as many as 32,000 branch shrines (bunsha) across Japan. The earliest structures of 711 stood on Inariyama and were relocated in 816 at the request of the monk Kūkai, the present main shrine building dates from 1499, and a drawing of 1786 records the two-storey entrance gate as the work of Toyotomi Hideyoshi. Visitors pass the rōmon and the honden and climb some four kilometres of mountain path to the inner shrine (okumiya) and to tens of thousands of privately dedicated rock altars (otsuka), walking beneath roughly 10,000 vermilion torii given by businesses, about 800 of which form the tunnel of the Senbon torii, a votive custom that spread from the Edo period; stone foxes (kitsune), Inari's messengers, hold the key to the rice granary in their mouths.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Torii_path_with_lantern_at_Fushimi_Inari_Taisha_Shrine%2C_Kyoto%2C_Japan.jpg/960px-Torii_path_with_lantern_at_Fushimi_Inari_Taisha_Shrine%2C_Kyoto%2C_Japan.jpg","caption":"The torii-lined path at Fushimi Inari-taisha, Kyoto. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Fushimi Inari-taisha","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fushimi_Inari-taisha"}]},
  {"name":"Sanzenin","kind":"temple","sect":"Tendai","founded":"Enryaku era (782–806)","lat":35.1422,"lon":135.8281,"note":"Sanzen-in is a Tendai monzeki temple at Ōhara, about an hour north of central Kyoto, founded by the monk Saichō, who introduced Tendai Buddhism to Japan in 804. Monzeki rank placed it among the small number of temples whose head priests were drawn from the imperial family, binding the house directly to the court. Its Heian-period triad of Amida Nyorai flanked by attendants is designated a National Treasure; the Ōjō Gokuraku-in, the oldest building on the grounds, was first raised in 985 and last rebuilt in 1143, and it holds the temple's most valued image, an Amida Buddha attended by Kannon on one side and Seishi on the other. Visitors pass first through the Kyakuden, a guest hall displaying calligraphy and painted sliding doors (fusuma), which opens onto the Shūheki-en garden with its small pond and hill, and then by corridor to the Shinden, where a further Amida stands flanked by Kannon and Fudō Myōō. From the Shinden the temple's best-known view opens across a moss garden studded with small stone figures towards the Ōjō Gokuraku-in among maple and cedar, at its finest in the autumn colours of mid-November; paths beyond lead to later halls, to rows of miniature Kannon donated by visitors, and to a treasure house.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Sanzen%27in_01.JPG/960px-Sanzen%27in_01.JPG","caption":"Sanzen-in at Ōhara, north of Kyoto. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Sanzen-in","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sanzen-in"}]},
  {"name":"Myōhō-ji","kind":"temple","sect":"Nichiren","founded":"1253 (Nichiren's hermitage; temple rebuilt 1357)","lat":35.3301,"lon":139.5467,"note":"Myōhō-ji, formally Ryōgonzan Renge-in Myōhō-ji, is a Nichiren temple in the Matsubagayatsu district of Kamakura, the Valley of Pine Needles, where Nichiren is held to have built his hut after coming to what was then the political and cultural centre of the country; by the temple's own records he settled here in 1253 and left for Minobu in 1272. Three temples in the valley (Ankokuron-ji, Chōshō-ji and Myōhō-ji) have contested that honour for centuries, and it was a ruling of the shogunate's courts in 1787 that granted Myōhō-ji the right to call itself the site of the hermitage. The ground was first occupied by a temple called Honkoku-ji, later transferred to Kyoto; Myōhō-ji was rebuilt in 1357 by its fifth chief abbot Nichiei, a natural son of Prince Morinaga, who dedicated it to his father, and it was protected and maintained by the Tokugawa and their vassals throughout the Edo period. Nichiei set two cenotaphs to his parents on the hill behind the main hall, and he and his mother Minami no Ōnkata are buried in the precinct, while Prince Morinaga, imprisoned by the Ashikaga in the cave now within Kamakura-gū and killed in 1335 at the age of 28, lies nearby at Nikaidō. The buildings are all kept closed to the public: a main hall holding a bone fragment revered as a relic of Nichiren and an effigy of Prince Morinaga, the Daigakuden with images of Shaka Nyorai, Katō Kiyomasa and Inari Myōjin, and the Hokkedō or Hall of Scriptures above; the temple's fame today rests on the fifty mossy steps that climb from the Niō gate, which have earned it the nickname Kokedera, the moss temple.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Myoho-ji_Main_Hall_Kamakura.jpg/960px-Myoho-ji_Main_Hall_Kamakura.jpg","caption":"The main hall of Myōhō-ji, Kamakura. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Myōhō-ji","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/My%C5%8Dh%C5%8D-ji"}]},
  {"name":"Kumano Nachi Taisha","kind":"shrine","sect":"Shinto","founded":"Ancient","lat":33.6686,"lon":135.8906,"note":"Kumano Nachi Taisha stands amid cedar forest in the Kii Mountains above Nachikatsuura in Wakayama Prefecture, enshrining Fusumi no kami, identified with Izanami no Mikoto, together with other deities. It is one of the three Kumano Sanzan shrines alongside Kumano Hongū Taisha and Kumano Hayatama Taisha, and pilgrims completed the Kumano circuit only by visiting all three, travelling the mountain roads now known collectively as the Kumano Kodō. The site is a clear case of Shintō and Buddhist syncretism (shinbutsu shūgō), the shrine standing beside the temple of Seiganto-ji, while the 133-metre Nachi Falls, worshipped at the nearby Hiryū Shrine, is believed to be inhabited by a kami called Hiryū Gongen. Between the shrine's heiden and Seiganto-ji grows a sacred camphor some 850 years old, said to have been planted by Taira no Shigemori (1138-1179) and girdled with a straw rope (shimenawa) and paper flags to mark it as a kami; its hollow base can be entered, where a small altar receives offerings. The shrine's principal festival is the Nachi-no-Hi Matsuri on 14 July, in which men dressed in white carry six-metre portable shrines and oversized torches whose fire symbolically purifies the waterfall, and Kumano Nachi Taisha is inscribed, with the other Kumano shrines and Kōya-san, in the UNESCO World Heritage Sacred Sites and Pilgrimage Routes in the Kii Mountain Range.","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Kumano-Nachi-shrine002.JPG/960px-Kumano-Nachi-shrine002.JPG","caption":"The heiden (offering hall) of Kumano Nachi Taisha, Wakayama Prefecture. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Kumano Nachi Taisha","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kumano_Nachi_Taisha"}]},
  {"name":"Nikkō Tōshō-gū","kind":"shrine","sect":"Shinto","founded":"1617","lat":36.7445,"lon":139.6167,"note":"Nikkō Tōshō-gū (日光東照宮) is the Shinto shrine dedicated to Tokugawa Ieyasu, founder of the Tokugawa shogunate, whose remains are entombed on the wooded slope above it; it was first built in 1617, while Ieyasu's son Hidetada was shōgun, by the Tokugawa retainer Tōdō Takatora, and was enlarged under the third shōgun, Iemitsu. Its role was as much political as devotional: throughout the Edo period the shogunate mounted stately processions from Edo to the shrine along the Nikkō Kaidō, journeys still reenacted at the spring and autumn festivals known as the processions of a thousand warriors, on a route lined by the cedars of the Cedar Avenue of Nikkō. The decoration is famously dense for a Shinto site: the Yōmeimon (陽明門) carries carvings in deep relief painted in rich colors and bears the nickname higurashi no mon no kei, meaning that one could look at it until sundown and not tire of seeing it; the next gate is the karamon, decorated with white ornaments, and nearby are the sleeping cat (Nemuri-neko) attributed to Hidari Jingorō and, on the stable of the sacred horses, the three wise monkeys who hear, speak and see no evil, a motif derived from a passage in the Analects. The five-storey pagoda, donated by a daimyō in 1650, burned down and was rebuilt in 1818; its storeys stand in ascending order for earth, water, fire, wind and aether, and its central shinbashira pillar hangs from chains to minimize damage from earthquakes. Visitors today climb hundreds of stone steps through the cryptomeria forest to Ieyasu's grave, where a torii bearing calligraphy attributed to Emperor Go-Mizunoo stands before the bronze urn holding his remains; forty-two of the shrine's structures are inscribed, together with Futarasan Shrine and Rinnō-ji, as the UNESCO World Heritage Site Shrines and Temples of Nikkō, five of them National Treasures of Japan and three more Important Cultural Properties (two swords held by the shrine are also National Treasures).","images":[{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/200801_Nikko_Tosho-gu_Nikko_Japan03s3.jpg/960px-200801_Nikko_Tosho-gu_Nikko_Japan03s3.jpg","caption":"The bronze torii of Nikkō Tōshō-gū, with the Yōmeimon gate beyond the stone steps. Wikimedia Commons."}],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Nikkō Tōshō-gū","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nikk%C5%8D_T%C5%8Dsh%C5%8D-g%C5%AB"}]}
];

